__One Ring Does _Not_ Rule Them All: Mitochondrial Population Genetics in Eukaryotic Pathogens__

Mitochondria, known as the powerhouse of the cell, are conventionally regarded as clonal, highly conserved, maternally inherited organelles carrying limited independent evolutionary signal within a species. Outside the metazoans, however, mitochondrial genetics is far less predictable, and the organelle itself is a defining, underexplored feature of eukaryotic pathogens, with no counterpart in bacteria or viruses. Here, we examine mitochondrial population genomic patterns across a pathogenic mold, yeast, and protozoan, to test this conventional assumption. Spanning different mating strategies, generation times, and selective pressures, these three species set a good foundation to explore how a genome already reduced to its functional minimums maintains that functionality under such divergent evolutionary paths. Using globally sampled, publicly available whole-genome sequencing data, we characterized mitochondrial variation within each species and evaluated the evidence for recombination against the expectation of strict clonal inheritance. We found that there is limited evidence to suggest that mitochondrial inheritance is not strictly clonal, although this evidence varied across species tested, indicating that there is no universal model of mitochondrial inheritance across eukaryotes, and much remains to be understood about the evolutionary dynamics of this genome throughout the eukaryotic supergroups.

